97,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,678,279,24438,3793,304,279,14461,315,198,59,9697,59,26888,90,17,92,488,1124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6364e41f4fc21957083edfc75cfc95384e3a03dd0171dc3d6c8af19ab06d63d1"},"seconds":47.43617868423462,"seed":782111872577741404,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we use the Binomial Theorem, which states:\n\\[\n(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k.\n\\]\nIn this problem, \\(a = \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(b = \\sqrt[3]{3}\\), and \\(n = 12\\). Therefore, the expansion is:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k.\n\\]\nA term in the expansion is rational if and only if both \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) are rational. This means that \\(12-k\\) must be even (so that \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is a power of 2) and \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 (so that \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is a power of 3).\n\nLet's find the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy these conditions. The possible values of \\(k\\) are those that are multiples of 3 and also make \\(12-k\\) even. The multiples of 3 up to 12 are 0, 3, 6, 9, and 12. We check each:\n- If \\(k = 0\\), then \\(12 - k = 12\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 3\\), then \\(12 - k = 9\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 6\\), then \\(12 - k = 6\\) (even).\n- If \\(k = 9\\), then \\(12 - k = 3\\) (odd).\n- If \\(k = 12\\), then \\(12 - k = 0\\) (even).\n\nSo the valid values of \\(k\\) are 0, 6, and 12. We now calculate the corresponding terms:\n- For \\(k = 0\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64.\n  \\]\n- For \\(k = 6\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = 924 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 66528.\n  \\]\n- For \\(k = 12\\):\n  \\[\n  \\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81.\n  \\]\n\nThe sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
